.Dd April 2, 2026
.Dt VI 1
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm vi
.Nd full-screen visual text editor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl RrsSv
.Op Fl c Ar command
.Op Fl t Ar tag
.Op Ar +command
.Op Ar file
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility is the full-screen visual frontend for Substrate's shared
.Pa usr.lib/exvi
editor core.
It provides a BSD-first
visual editing interface over the same buffer, command, tag, recovery,
and option
machinery used by
.Xr ex 1 .
.Pp
Normal mode, insert mode, replace mode, search prompts, and visual
.Ql \&:
command entry are supported.
The visual command line exposes the same documented command set and
.Ic :set
options as standalone
.Xr ex 1 .
.Pp
The current visual frontend supports:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It movement
.Cm h
.Cm j
.Cm k
.Cm l ,
.Cm 0 ,
.Cm ^ ,
.Cm $ ,
.Cm _
.Cm g_
.Cm gg ,
.Cm G ,
.Cm +
.Cm - ,
.Cm H ,
.Cm M ,
.Cm L ,
.Cm Ctrl-F ,
.Cm Ctrl-B ,
.Cm Ctrl-D ,
.Cm Ctrl-U ,
.Cm Ctrl-E ,
.Cm Ctrl-Y ,
.Cm zz ,
.Cm z<Enter> ,
.Cm z-
.Ql z.
.Cm zt ,
.Cm zb
.It word and text motions
.Cm w
.Cm b
.Cm e ,
.Cm W
.Cm B
.Cm E ,
.Cm ge ,
.Cm gE ,
.Ql (
.Ql ) ,
.Ql {
.Ql } ,
.Cm [[
.Cm ]]
.Cm []
.Cm ][ ,
.Cm %
.Cm *
.Cm #
.It find and mark motions
.Cm f
.Cm F
.Cm t
.Cm T ,
.Ql ; ,
.Ql , ,
.Cm ' Ns Ar mark
.Cm ` Ns Ar mark ,
.Cm m Ns Ar mark
.It search and prompt entry
.Ql /
.Ql ?
.Cm n
.Cm N
.Ql :
.It editing and operators
.Cm i
.Cm a
.Cm o
.Cm O ,
.Cm x
.Cm X ,
.Cm s
.Cm S ,
.Cm r
.Cm R ,
.Cm C
.Cm D
.Cm Y
.Cm J
.Cm ~ ,
line shifts with
.Cm >>
and
.Cm <<
and delete, change, and yank with the supported motion families
.It put, undo, and redo
.Cm p
.Cm P ,
.Cm u ,
.Cm Ctrl-R
.It tag and mode handoff
.Ql Ctrl-]
.Cm Ctrl-T ,
.Cm Q ,
.Cm Ctrl-O
for one-shot normal commands from insert or replace mode
.El
.Pp
Counts are supported across a large subset of motions and operator-pending
commands.
The current implementation still has documented parity gaps in a few motion,
undo, and repeat corner cases; see
.Pa docs/specs/exvi.md
for the tracked conformance backlog.
.Pp
A search pattern, as an ex command's, is a POSIX basic regular expression
.Pq Xr regex 3 ,
with
.Ql \e<
and
.Ql \e>
matching at the start and end of a word, and
.Ql ~
standing for the replacement text of the last
.Ic :substitute .
With the
.Ic magic
option off,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&[ ,
.Ql *
and
.Ql ~
are ordinary characters and their escaped forms are the special ones.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl R
Start in readonly mode.
Plain writes are rejected unless forced with
.Ql \&! .
.It Fl r
Recover the named file from a preserved recovery image.
.It Fl s
Suppress interactive diagnostics where possible, and read no startup
commands from
.Ev EXINIT ,
.Pa ~/.exrc
or
.Pa ./.exrc .
Commands given with
.Fl c
still run.
.It Fl S
Start in secure mode.
Shell escapes and shell-capable commands are disabled.
.It Fl v
Accepted for shared parser compatibility.
When invoking
.Nm
directly it keeps visual mode active.
.It Fl c Ar command
Execute
.Ar command
after startup processing.
This option may be specified more than once.
.It Fl t Ar tag
Start by editing the file and location named by
.Ar tag .
.It Ar +command
Execute
.Ar command
after startup processing.
Common forms include
.Ql +42
to move to line 42 and
.Ql +next
to advance within the argument list.
.It Ar file
Open
.Ar file
as the current buffer.
If multiple files are given, they become the initial argument list.
.El
.Sh SET OPTIONS
The shared
.Ic :set
option table currently supports:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ic number , nu
Display line numbers in the visual gutter.
.It Ic list , li
Display tabs and line endings in list form.
.It Ic autoindent , ai
Reuse current-line indentation for
.Cm o ,
.Cm O ,
split-newline, and linewise change/substitute helpers.
.It Ic ignorecase , ic
Make search, repeat-search, and word-search commands case-insensitive.
.It Ic readonly , ro
Mark the session readonly.
.It Ic showmode , smd
Show or suppress insert and replace mode banners.
.It Ic wrapscan , ws
Wrap searches at buffer boundaries.
.It Ic magic
Give
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&[ ,
.Ql *
and
.Ql ~
their special meaning in patterns.
It is on by default.
.It Ic writeany , wa
Let
.Ic :write
replace an existing file other than the current one without
.Ql \&! .
It is off by default.
.It Ic exrc , ex
Read
.Pa ./.exrc
at startup.
It is off by default and has effect only when set by
.Ev EXINIT
or
.Pa ~/.exrc .
.It Ic tabstop= , ts=
Set tab rendering width.
The default is 8.
.It Ic scroll= , sc=
Set the half-page scroll amount after it is explicitly configured.
.It Ic tags=
Set the comma-separated tag-file search path used by
.Ql Ctrl-]
and
.Ic :tag .
.It Ic directory= , dir=
The directory under which the user's recovery directory,
.Pa exvi- Ns Ar uid ,
is kept.
The default is
.Pa /var/tmp .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ev EXINIT
If set, supplies startup commands, and
.Pa ~/.exrc
is not loaded.
Ignored with
.Fl s .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa ~/.exrc
Per-user startup commands, loaded when
.Ev EXINIT
is not set, and only when ownership and directory safety checks pass.
.It Pa ./.exrc
Per-directory startup commands, loaded after
.Ev EXINIT
or
.Pa ~/.exrc
only if one of them set the
.Ic exrc
option, only when the ownership and directory safety checks pass, and not
when it is
.Pa ~/.exrc
itself.
Neither
.Pa .exrc
is loaded with
.Fl s
or
.Fl S .
.It Pa tags
Default tag file name when the
.Ic tags
option is left at its default value.
Each line is a tag name, a file name and an address, separated by tabs.
The address is a line number or a
.Li / Ns Ar pattern Ns Li /
or
.Li \&? Ns Ar pattern Ns Li \&?
search, and nothing else: any other address is reported as a bad tag
address, never run as a command.
.It Pa /var/tmp/exvi- Ns Ar uid
The user's recovery directory, under the
.Ic directory
option's value: preserved recovery images, one per file, written by
.Ic :preserve
and for sessions ended by
.Dv SIGHUP
or
.Dv SIGTERM .
It is created with mode 0700 and used only while it is the user's, not a
symbolic link and closed to everyone else; each image is created afresh
with mode 0600, and only an image the user owns is recovered.
Writing the file removes an image only if this session wrote or recovered
from it.
.El
.Sh ASYNCHRONOUS EVENTS
In visual mode
.Ql Ctrl-C
is an ordinary key.
A
.Dv SIGINT
sent to
.Nm
acts as
.Aq ESC :
it ends text input, and cancels a partial command or a
.Ql \&:
or search prompt.
.Dv SIGHUP
and
.Dv SIGTERM
preserve a modified buffer and end the session.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits 0 on success and with a value greater than 0 if an error occurs.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vi file.txt
vi +42 file.txt
vi -R file.txt
vi -t main tags-demo.c
vi -c 'set number' file.txt
.Ed
.Sh NOTES
The supported Substrate contract is intentionally narrower than the union of
historical BSD, nvi, and Vim behavior.
Only the command names, abbreviations, motions, options, and entry points
documented here and in
.Pa docs/specs/exvi.md
are part of the supported interface.
.Pp
Readonly and restricted entry points such as
.Cm view
and
.Cm rvi
are implemented through the shared frontend parser even though they are not
separate binaries today.
.Pp
The current renderer still uses a repaint-heavy refresh strategy rather than a
dirty-region diff engine.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ex 1 ,
.Xr regex 3
